{
  "result": "Morning arrives with a hush, as though the sky is holding its breath. The first light drips over rooftops, painting angles of concrete in watercolor pink. Sparrows rearrange their conversations, stitching loose syllables between telephone wires. I brew coffee not for caffeine but for the ceremony: the bloom of steam, the hollow clink of a spoon against porcelain. Each dawn feels like an unwrapped gift whose ribbon is still curling. Yesterday\u2019s complications wait beyond the threshold, polite houseguests reclining in shadows, reluctant to reassert themselves. For a few minutes everything belongs to possibility. I step outside, barefoot, onto the cool balcony. A distant train exhales a single, elongated note that seems to underline the horizon. The city, still rubbing sleep from its eyes, allows secrets to float upward: the faint aroma of baking bread, the purr of a newspaper truck, the click of a cyclist\u2019s freewheel. I think about how every ordinary thing carries an extraordinary history\u2014minerals turned into gears, sunlight fermented into cherries, syllables pressed into paper. Wonder is never far; we misplace it among errands, calendars, and glazed screens. So I make a quiet pact with this tender sliver of morning: to keep my senses unraveled, to greet the day like an amateur astronomer greeting a comet, neck craned, eyes watering, heart beating ahead of comprehension. The cup warms my palms, and I remember that being alive is already the rarest phenomenon I will ever study. The sun climbs, unobtrusive, and the day begins without asking permission.",
